# mix-of-capabilities §13 — 13×14×08×15: le ore fuori dal frigorifero contro il
# limite della classe dell'alimento (USDA FSIS, «Danger Zone»: mai piu' di 2 ore).
# Fatti insegnati parlando e salvati: il limite dei deperibili, e quali alimenti lo
# sono. Entro il limite non si dice «sicuro».
[mock live]
!set PARROT0_SESSION=
!set PARROT0_PROFILE=kb/profiles/agi.p0
!set PARROT0_LANG=en
!reset

[test past the limit of the food's class]
!timeout 30
> I left cooked chicken out for 3 hours, is it safe?
< No: chicken is a perishable, and a perishable should not be left out for more than 2 hours; 3 hours is past that, so it is safer to throw it away.

[test within the limit is not called safe]
!timeout 30
> the rice was left on the counter for one hour, can I still eat it?
<~ It is within the limit
<~ Put it in the fridge now
<! safe

[test a thing that is not food gets no verdict]
!timeout 30
> I left my keys out for 3 hours
<! No:

[test the temperature said in the turn changes the limit and the verdict]
!timeout 30
> I left the chicken out for 2 hours, is it safe?
<~ It is within the limit
> I left the chicken out for 2 hours at 35 degrees, is it safe?
< No: at 35 degrees a perishable like chicken should not be left out for more than 1 hour; 2 hours is past that, so it is safer to throw it away.

[test mix 08x13x15 the internal temperature against the food's safe threshold]
!timeout 30
> I cooked the chicken to 68 degrees, is it safe?
< Not yet: the safe internal temperature of chicken is 74 degrees, and 68 is 6 degrees short. Keep cooking and measure again in the thickest part.
> the thermometer reads 72 degrees in the ground beef, is it done?
< It has reached the safe internal temperature of ground beef: 71 degrees, as long as you measured it in the thickest part.
